% ============================================================================
% phoneme-substitution — changing one sound in a word to form a new word, a
% FIFTH literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from all four prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING (RF.K.2.a), `syllable-count.adj`
% recalls a SYLLABLE COUNT (RF.K.2.b), `initial-sound.adj` recalls a
% BEGINNING sound (RF.K.2.d), and `onset-rime.adj` recalls an ONSET/RIME
% split (RF.K.2.c). This library recalls what happens when you SUBSTITUTE
% one sound in a word for another, grounding RF.K.2.e ("Add or substitute
% individual sounds in simple, one-syllable words to make new words") as a
% pure lookup: `phoneme_substitution(original_word, original_sound,
% new_sound, new_word)`. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? phoneme_substitution(make, m, b, $New)   % bake
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a FOUR-column table): each `row` lowers
% to a ground relation `phoneme_substitution(original_word, original_sound,
% new_sound, new_word)` carrying the Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the new word
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% substitution not the one the cited page walks through. The query also
% runs BACKWARD — bind the new sound and new word and recall the original:
%
%     ? phoneme_substitution($Orig, $OrigSound, b, bake)   % make, m
%
% The one substitution this table carries, as a little truth table:
%
%     original_word   original_sound   new_sound   new_word
%     -------------   --------------   ---------   --------
%     make            m                b           bake
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module (the SAME page
% `syllable-count.adj` already cites, a different section of it),
% WebFetch-verified TWICE for consistency before writing. The exact spans:
%
%   "I can change one sound in a word to form a new word. Watch me. I will
%    change 'make' to 'bake'."
%   "The first sound in make is /m/. The first sound in bake is /b/."
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier `syllable-count.adj`'s sibling section
% of this page already earned. Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE
% substitution the cited page walks through step by step — honestly narrow
% (mirroring `onset-rime.adj`'s and `initial-sound.adj`'s precedent) rather
% than inventing a substitution the source does not demonstrate. A future
% slice can add a second substitution once a comparably clean citation is
% found. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table phoneme_substitution {
    columns original_word, original_sound, new_sound, new_word

    row (make, m, b, bake)

    source "I can change one sound in a word to form a new word. Watch me. I will change 'make' to 'bake'."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
